From ac525a6780efb091625364702900f623ae0c4dda Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Angelo Arifi <arifilabs@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 12:06:37 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] docs(r57): registry rows for the admit switch and for the n=7
 cliff it uncovered

Two rows. The switch's own row states the inertness as a source-level fact (unset =>
the helper returns the inherited constant and the added conjunct is trivially true),
carries the 71/71 correctness at both arms, and points the perf pairing at the lane
report rather than at a receipt that does not exist yet.

The second row is an OPEN ITEM with no switch behind it: q4_K MMVQ loses 34.6% at
k=2048 and 27.4% at k=3072 on the n=6->7 step, against 6% at k=5120, at working sets
50% apart - width-locked, i.e. the NUM_COLS == 7 tail defect R46 named and R48b removed
for S-X8, still alive on q4_K and never measured there. Out of lane-248's scope; the
row exists so the next lane finds the receipts instead of re-discovering the cliff.

Origin: fork-authored (ArifiLabs lane-248 R57 q4_K cheaper dot, Opus 5 MEDIUM kernel-maker)
Measured-effect: documentation only - no code path touched, no default moves
Lane: lane-248
Cherry-picked-from: 589ddebc4 (SOURCE PATHS ONLY, ArifiLabs lane-254 R66 integration)
Recut-onto: 1b6185951 (ArifiLabs lane-254 R66c, from 8c6bb5b5b)
---
 docs/OPTIONS-REGISTRY.md | 2 ++
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+)

diff --git a/docs/OPTIONS-REGISTRY.md b/docs/OPTIONS-REGISTRY.md
index 7b7c46685b..35ef68a436 100644
--- a/docs/OPTIONS-REGISTRY.md
+++ b/docs/OPTIONS-REGISTRY.md
@@ -41,6 +41,8 @@ safe measurement posture.
 | `GGML_ARIFI_G128_MMV_ROWS` | **unset = the shipped value (2 rows).** Any positive integer overrides rows-per-workgroup for the `Q2_0_G128` Vulkan mul_mat_vec pipeline only. Added 2026-07-30 (lane-110G). | Scope: the **integer-dot** (`q8_1`) `mul_mat_vec` registration for `GGML_TYPE_Q2_0_G128` and nothing else. That is the registration this format actually uses — `ggml_vk_should_use_mmvq()` returns true for it on AMD at `k >= 2048` — and it is a different knob from the two *dequant* registrations, which take rows from `rm_stdq`. Scoped to g128 rather than raising `rm_kq_int` itself, which is shared with `Q2_0`, `Q2_K` and several K-quants this box has no evidence for. | **Do not turn it on.** It exists so the question could be asked on one binary with an env flip instead of two builds, and the answer was no. Keep it for re-asking on other hardware, where `rm_kq_int` is equally untuned. | **MEASURED 2026-07-30, and it is a NULL — the shipped value wins.** Which pipeline is live was established first, by measurement rather than by reading: `GGML_VK_DISABLE_MMVQ` moves decode **34.11 → 28.39 tok/s (+20.1%, ranges DISJOINT**, three interleaved replicates, one binary), so the integer-dot path is the one serving this format. Its rows come from `2 * rm_kq_int`, and **`rm_kq_int` is 1 on every device — no vendor branch raises it** (AMD_GCN raises `rm_stdq`/`rm_kq`/`rm_stdq_int`, Intel raises `rm_stdq`/`rm_stdq_int`), so this pipeline has run 2 rows per workgroup everywhere it has ever run.<br>**Sweep** (RIG-A, `Ternary-Bonsai-8B-Q2_0.g128`, `-ngl 99 -c 2048`, 519-token prefill, `n_predict 128`, three interleaved replicates, control reproduced within 0%): decode **2 rows `34.17`** · 4 rows `33.59` (**−1.7%, OVERLAPPING**) · 8 rows `33.38` (**−2.3%, OVERLAPPING**). Neither is a result; the untuned default is already right here.<br>**Correctness held**: generated output is byte-identical across all three row counts, which matters because a wrong row count in this kernel is silent wrong math rather than a crash.<br>**One unexplained measurement, kept rather than smoothed:** *prompt* at 4 rows collapses to `174.63` from `310.85` (tight range, not noise) while 8 rows returns to `291.12` — non-monotonic and not accounted for. The prediction that prompt would be flat was wrong: `should_use_mmvq` also returns true for `n > 1`, so this pipeline serves small batches and does touch prefill. Evidence: `research/local-inference/trial-evidence/110/g128-kernels/rows/` and `.../mmvq/`. |
 | `GGML_ARIFI_Q6K_XFOLD` | **ON when unset** (`=0` opts out; any other value keeps it on). Specialization constant 4 of `mul_mat_vec_q6_k.comp`, passed at both Q6_K dmmv registrations (`ggml-vulkan.cpp:7100` f32_f32, `:7166` f16_f32). Added 2026-09-14 (lane-236 / R48c). One binary serves both arms. | Scope: the **f32 dequant** `mul_mat_vec` path for `GGML_TYPE_Q6_K` and nothing else. Arm 1 does two things: (a) loads the superblock's four `data_b` `vec4`s once per superblock instead of once per row, reusing them across all `NUM_ROWS` rows; (b) removes q6_K's per-weight `- 32` bias from the decode and applies it as one `fma` per sub-block per column, `sum(b*u) - 32*sum(b)`. (b) carries its own n-GATE at `NUM_COLS <= 3` — counted, it removes 16 subtractions per row but adds 4 fma per row **per column**, so it inverts at width 5+. (a) has no crossover. Orthogonal to `GGML_VK_Q6K_DIRECT_SCALES`: the fold is applied identically in both of that constant's arms. | **Do not change it without a pairing.** It was registered ON on counted load and ALU deltas (n=1: −13.3% loads, −8.7% ALU); it **stays** ON on the measured pairing in the next cell — no q6_K row below −3% at any width, plus the n=6 dip removal — not on a width-1 win, which the pairing did not deliver on both FFN shapes. Set `=0` to back it out in the field without a rebuild — the intended recovery if a width regresses, since arm 0 is the shipped order byte for byte. | **MEASURED 2026-09-14 (lane-236 / R48c), and it is kept on no-harm plus one dip removal, NOT on a width-1 win.** Pairing: `research/local-inference/lane-evidence/2026-09-14-lane-234-r48-kernels/32-paired-r48cq6k.txt`, 3 quiet interleaved rounds, one binary env-flipped (arm A = `GGML_ARIFI_Q6K_XFOLD=0`, arm B = default ON), `test-backend-ops perf -o MUL_MAT -b Vulkan0` at the three 27B decode shapes plus legacy4096, n = 1..8. Result on the q6_K rows: **n = 1..5, 7, 8 is a wash, span −3.0% to +5.6%** (best +5.61% ffn-down n=1; worst −2.97% legacy4096 n=4, −2.91% legacy4096 n=8); **n = 6 is +26% to +38%** (+38.03% embd-out, +27.53% ffn-down, +26.28% ffn-gate-up, 3/3 rounds each) — arm A has a dip at `NUM_COLS == 6` (embd-out 31.10 GB/s at n=6 against 67.16 at n=5 and 53.50 at n=7) that arm B removes. **No q6_K row is below −3%.** Controls: `q4_K` and `q5_K` shaders are untouched and their three rows below −3% have overlapping per-round triples (noise, reported unfiltered). **Acceptance rule 1 of the report (§6: ≥ +5% median at n=1 on BOTH FFN shapes) is NOT met** — ffn-down +5.61% passes, ffn-gate-up **+3.69%** does not. The row is kept on rule 2 (veto: no q6_K row worse than −3%; worst −2.97%) plus the n=6 dip removal. Correctness: `test-backend-ops -o MUL_MAT` clean in all four constant arms (1688/1688 at XFOLD=1; the single 248320x5120 n=1 failure seen at XFOLD=0 is a pre-existing intermittent defect that also fires 3/60 on the pure-upstream shader path, `r48c-evidence/42-*`, not this arm's). **Not bit-identical to arm 0** — the `-32` moves out of the fma chain into a single `fma(-32.0, xs, sum)`, which reorders float sums; `FLOAT_TYPE` is `float` for every mat-vec pipeline (`vulkan-shaders-gen.cpp:846`), never `float16_t`, so the cancellation is in fp32 and expected well inside the MUL_MAT NMSE gate. **Named risk:** arm 1 holds `NUM_COLS x 4` `vec4`s live across the row loop (128 VGPRs of activation at `NUM_COLS = 8`) — the exact shape that spilled in lane-230, `mul_mat_vec_sx8.comp:60-71`, losing −45% at n=5 and −77% at n=8. That risk did not materialise: the widest rows (n=7, n=8) are inside the wash band. Pairing plan and acceptance bars: `OPUS-R48C-Q6K-MATVEC-REPORT.md` §6, which also records that rule 1 was not met. |
 | `GGML_ARIFI_Q6K_MMVQ` | **unset = the shipped route** (MMVQ for Q6_K on Intel only). `=1` forces Q6_K onto the q8_1 integer-dot MMVQ path on any vendor; `=0` forces it off even on Intel. Added 2026-09-14 (lane-236 / R48c) in `ggml_vk_should_use_mmvq()`, `ggml-vulkan.cpp:~11724`. | Scope: the route decision for `GGML_TYPE_Q6_K` only. It flips one `bool`; it registers no pipeline. The `mul_mat_vec_q6_k_q8_1_f32` pipeline it reaches is **already registered unconditionally** (`:7211`), so this changes which existing shader runs, not what is built. | **DIAGNOSTIC ONLY — do not ship it on.** It exists to settle one question on one binary: at n=1, k>=2048 on AMD, `ggml_vk_should_use_mmvq()` returns **true** for Q4_K and Q5_K (vendor switch, `:11841-11850`) and **false** for Q6_K (Intel-only early return, `:11724`). So the historical "Q6_K 41.0 GB/s vs q5_K 63.8 / q4_K 72.5" compares an **f32-dequant** kernel against two **integer-dot** kernels. `=1` makes the comparison like-for-like. | **MEASURED 2026-09-14 (lane-236 / R48c) as a DIAGNOSTIC, and it stays OFF. The default does not move.** Pairing: `research/local-inference/lane-evidence/2026-09-14-lane-234-r48-kernels/32-paired-r48cq6kroute.txt`, same protocol as the row above (3 quiet interleaved rounds, one binary env-flipped, arm B = `GGML_ARIFI_Q6K_MMVQ=1`), against an arm A that carries the R48c activation hoist but **no A-hoist inside the MMVQ path**. Result: at **n <= 4** it is a wash to +5% on ffn-down and legacy4096 (+5.53% / +5.29% best) but already **−3.28% at n=1 and −12.11% at n=4 on embd-out**; at **n >= 5 it collapses on every shape, −46% to −143%** (worst −142.52%, embd-out n=5: 61.71 GB/s on the f32-dequant path against 25.44 GB/s on MMVQ). Correctness on the route: **43/43 OK** (`r48c-evidence/12-mulmat-q6k-mmvq-route.txt`, rc 0; the 8 `type_b=f16` rows are declined by the CPU reference, see below). So the like-for-like question is answered — the integer-dot route does **not** recover the q5_K gap on this box — and **the Q6_K default stays the f32-dequant mat-vec**. A later A-hoist inside the MMVQ shader (R48b) could change the n >= 5 picture; nothing here licenses turning it on. Upstream's stated reason for restricting this to Intel is that "q6_k only has 2-byte alignment which makes it somewhat problematic" (`:11724`) — a real property: `block_q6_K` is **210 bytes** (`types.glsl:452`), so no `packed32` view of it is even representable (an aligned-4 member forces array stride `roundUp(210,4) = 212 != 210`), and `210*ib mod 4` alternates 0/2 so a raw u32 alias is unaligned on half of all blocks. This flag does **not** claim upstream is wrong; it makes the cost measurable on the 780M. **Run its correctness filter before its perf filter.** |
+| `GGML_ARIFI_MMV_MAX_COLS` | **unset = the inherited routing** (`mul_mat_vec_max_cols = 8`: mat-vec at n<=8, GEMM above). `=k` admits mat-vec only up to n=k and hands wider ones to `ggml_vk_mul_mat_q_f16()`; `=0` puts EVERY width including n=1 on the GEMM path. Added 2026-09-17 (lane-248 / R57) as `ggml_vk_mmv_max_cols()`, `ggml-vulkan.cpp:~481`, read at the route (`:~13190`) and by the perf-logger's `_VEC` label so a receipt names the path actually taken. | Scope: which of two EXISTING code paths a MUL_MAT takes. It registers no pipeline and compiles no shader. Default-inert by construction: with the variable unset the helper returns the constant and the added `> 0` conjunct is trivially true, so the condition is the inherited one term for term. | **DIAGNOSTIC.** It exists because the drafted line's multiplier is the cost of one extra VERIFY ROW (R63 §3a: 43.0 ms = 0.177 plain-token-equivalents, which caps the line at 10.9 t/s with a perfect drafter at n-max 4), and that cost could not be compared across regimes: below 9 columns the GEMM path was unreachable, so no width the served line actually uses had ever been timed on both. | **Correctness 2026-09-17 (lane-248 / R57): 71/71 OK at the default AND at `=0`, rc 0** — `r57-evidence/20-mulmat-q4k-default.txt`, `20-mulmat-q4k-gemm0.txt` (`test -o MUL_MAT -p "type_a=q4_K"`, which covers n=1..8, odd m=1023/6271/6273 and both 27B decode shapes). n=1 on the GEMM path had never been reachable on this fork before and is correct. Perf pairing: this lane's report §4 (`OPUS-R57-Q4K-CHEAPER-DOT-REPORT.md`). **No default moves on any device**, and served t/s is UNMEASURED by this lane. |
+| **OPEN ITEM (no switch) — q4_K MMVQ `NUM_COLS == 7` cliff at small k** | n/a: this is a measured defect on a shipped path, recorded here so it is not re-discovered | On q4_K 248320-row mat-vec under forced MMVQ, the n=6→7 step costs **+34.6% at k=2048** and **+27.4% at k=3072**, against **+6% at k=5120** (`r57-evidence/13-knee-table.txt`, `13-knee3072-table.txt`, lane-248 / R57, 3 interleaved rounds each, one hold, `cells=8` per round). Width-locked at n=7 across two k whose activation working sets differ by 50% (15.8 vs 23.6 KB), so it is **not** a capacity crossing. | This is the `NUM_COLS == 7` tail defect R46 named in the mmvq framework (the `GGML_ARIFI_SX8_MMVQ` block in `ggml-vulkan.cpp` predicted it; R48b's A-side hoist removed it for S-X8). It is still alive on **q4_K** at small k and had never been measured there. **Not chased by lane-248** — out of that lane's scope, which was the width-4 knee. | UNFIXED. A lane that takes it should start from the two receipts above; the cheapest discriminator is whether the `NUM_COLS == 7` pipeline's spec-constant tail differs from 6 and 8 in register pressure or in the partial-tail branch, at a k where the cliff is large. No default moves until someone measures it. |
 | `LANE110_PROF=1` | unset | Emits per-operation timing for pipeline initialization, scheduling, build tasks, and forward execution. | Diagnosis only. Disable for benchmark runs. | Pipeline-init reuse itself reduced rebuild work from `2472 µs` to `0–2 µs`; profiler overhead has not been independently quantified. |
 | `LANE110_PREFETCH_CAP` | implementation default, currently `3*n_expert_used` | Limits standalone expert prefetch aggressiveness. | Sweep under cache pressure after establishing the baseline. | Recorded server sequences: default approximately `[5.07/9.03/9.58/12.01]`; cap `1` `[5.13/9.88/10.29/11.22]`; cap `96` `[5.79/11.73/12.19/11.26]`. Default retained because apparent ramp differences require controlled retest. |
 | `--ctx-checkpoints 0` | `0` | Disables server prompt rewind/checkpoints. | Keep off unless a workload demonstrably requires rewind semantics. | Largest historical gain: stock rewind `39.9/68.4/280`; ON_DEVICE checkpoint `84.8/128.8/341.6`; disabled approximately `101/150/372` across short/medium/long prefill. |
